# CHAPTER II

## PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES

#### Article 2

Protection of women victims of violence shall be conducted based on the principles of:  a. respect for the rights of victims;  
b. justice and gender equality;  
c. protection of victims;  
d. best interests of victims; and  e. non-discrimination.

#### Article 3

Protection of women and children victims of violence aims to:  a. prevent all forms of violence against women and children that occur within the household and/or community;  
b. protect and provide security for women and children;  
c. provide legal assistance;  
d. provide services to women and children -8- victims of violence, including reporting, and to reporters and witnesses;  
e. seek recovery and social reintegration; and f. increase community participation.

# CHAPTER III

## SCOPE

#### Article 4

The scope of protection for women and children victims of violence includes the rights of victims, obligations and authority of the Regional Government, implementation of protection, reporting, control, guidance and supervision, community participation and financing.

# CHAPTER IV

## RIGHTS OF VICTIMS

#### Article 5

1. Every woman and child victim of violence has the right to:  a. obtain protection for personal, family and property security, and be free from threats related to statements that will be, are being, or have been given;  
b. participate in the process of selecting and determining the form of protection and security support;  
c. be free from ensnaring questions;  
d. obtain information regarding case developments and court decisions;  e. receive services that are fast, appropriate, comfortable, and according to needs;  
f. receive recovery, social reintegration, continuous treatment up to rehabilitation and empowerment;  
g. receive assistance at every level of examination and during the judicial process and out-of-court processes carried out; and h. receive psychological assistance, spiritual guidance, economic, social and interpreter services;  
2. The rights of victims as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.  

#### Article 6

In addition to the rights as referred to in Article 5, every victim must:  a. provide true information to the parties -9- concerned;  
b. become a witness if it does not endanger oneself and if there is a security guarantee for the victim

# CHAPTER V

## OBLIGATIONS AND AUTHORITY OF THE REGIONAL GOVERNMENT

#### Article 7

1. The Regional Government is obligated to: a. prevent the occurrence of violence;  
b. provide protection for victims of violence;  
c. provide recovery services and social reintegration;  
d. encourage and increase community participation;  
e. cooperate with service providers in efforts to prevent, protect and recover victims of violence.  f. facilitate the establishment of P2TP2A based on Hospitals, Community Health Centers, Trauma Center Shelters, and other supporting institutions; and g. supervise the implementation of services for victims, with service standards involving the community.  
2. To carry out the obligations as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government has the authority to: a. formulate policies and programs on the elimination of violence against women and children;  
b. establish P2TP2A;  
c. facilitate the implementation of P2TP2A activities;  
d. provide facilities and infrastructure;  
e. increase the capacity of service provider institutions;  

f. coordinate and cooperate in the implementation of protection for victims of violence against women and children;  
g. encourage community participation; and h. conduct monitoring and evaluation.  
3. The Regional Government in carrying out the obligations as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) must consider the rights and obligations of parents, guardians, husbands, wives or other persons who are legally responsible for the victim.

##### CHAPTER VI

##### IMPLEMENTATION OF PROTECTION

## Part One

Institutional -10-

#### Article 8

In implementing protection for women and children victims of violence, the Regional Government is assisted by P2TP2A.

#### Article 9

(1) P2TP2A as referred to in Article 8 shall be established by the Regional Government in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.  (2) P2TP2A as referred to in paragraph (1) consists of elements of the Regional Government, non-governmental institutions, health service institutions, law enforcement officials, professional personnel, volunteer assistants, social workers, religious leaders, shelters, and social rehabilitation centers.  

3. The membership of P2TP2A as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be determined by Regent's Decree.

#### Article 10

1. The implementation of P2TP2A shall be coordinated by the SKPD in charge of women's empowerment and child protection.  (2) Further provisions regarding procedures, requirements, and establishment of P2TP2A as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be regulated by Regent's Regulation.

#### Article 11

P2TP2A as referred to in Article 8 has the duty to:
a. seek prevention;  
b. seek recovery and social reintegration;  
c. provide legal protection;  
d. conduct coordination;  
e. seek to increase community participation, and f. monitoring and reporting.

### Paragraph 1

Prevention Efforts

#### Article 12

1. Prevention efforts as referred to in Article 11 letter a include: a. forming a network for prevention and handling of violence through coordination, integration, synchronization of violence prevention based on partnership patterns;  
b. establishing a violence prevention system, mapping locations or -11- areas prone to violence;  
c. conducting communication, information, and education about violence against women and children; and d. socializing laws and regulations related to violence against women and children.  
2. Prevention efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out in an integrated manner coordinated by the SKPD having duties and functions in the field of women's empowerment.

#### Article 13

In addition to prevention efforts carried out by the Regional Government as referred to in Article 12, prevention efforts must also be carried out by:
a. family and closest relatives;  
b. community and business sector; and

c. educational institutions.  

Paragraph 2 Recovery and Social Reintegration Efforts

#### Article 14

Recovery and social reintegration efforts as referred to in Article 11 letter b include:
a. providing physical recovery at health service institutions;  
b. providing medicolegal services;  
c. assisting the return of victims;  
d. providing temporary protection in shelters;  
e. providing psychosocial recovery and assistance;  
f. providing spiritual guidance services; and g. preparing families and communities, economic empowerment, and return to school and/or other educational institutions.  

Paragraph 3 Legal Protection

#### Article 15

Legal protection as referred to in Article 11 letter c includes:
a. providing protection in shelters;  
b. providing assistance in the legal process; and c. providing special legal protection for child victims of violence may be carried out by appointing guardianship in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

12- Paragraph 4 Coordination

#### Article 16

Coordination as referred to in Article 11 letter d includes:  a. coordinating the handling of violence cases with provincial integrated services;  
b. coordinating with integrated services in other regions;  
c. coordinating with service provider institutions for women and children victims of violence; and d. coordinating and consulting with the Regional Government.

### Paragraph 5

Efforts to Increase Community Participation
